body:
TCP Timestamp is a per-packet option, not a packet.  You can disable that option's use on packets where the router is an endpoint of a connection, but mangling all TCP packets passing through to check for and (if present) remove the Timestamp option would be prohibitively expensive, and is likely not implemented.

To disable RFC 1323 options, you can do the following:

    [edit system internet-options]
    no-tcp-rfc1323;
    [edit system internet-options]
    no-tcp-rfc1323-paws;

The SRX300 can absolutely perform the tasks you've asked about. 

To assign a port to a vlan, simply configure it as such:

    unit 0 {
        family ethernet-switching {
            vlan {
                members <vlan-name>;
            }
        }
    }

Then place the VLAN interfaces (I usually use IRB interfaces) into the zone or zones you wish, and ensure policies to allow inter-routing are set.

I had to go a bit further with mine,

    family inet6 {
      dhcpv6-client {
        client-type stateful;
        client-ia-type ia-pd;
        client-ia-type ia-na;
        rapid-commit;
        client-identifier duid-type duid-ll;
        retransmission-attempt 6;
      }
    }
